  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-{x}^{2})\\,i+x\\sqrt{1-|y|}\\,j\\,.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-{x}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=x\\sqrt{1-|y|}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}>0\\,\u27f9\\,-1<x<1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100214/assets/89c78ec587826c15c90ef4ffdfc0fd9f8af3d7b4d3d1cc099ce24b883b9842c2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-|y|\\ge 0\\,\u27f9\\,-1\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100214/assets/addd649aa739711fd34ebd3e82579c8c0100a2b9a109d5cafabde750bc94eb9a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100214/assets/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png)",
